<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-vector-spaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vector Spaces</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Every argument we have made about <m>\R^n</m> used only a handful of properties of
      addition and scalar multiplication. Promoting that handful to a definition lets the
      same theory apply to polynomials, matrices, and much else. The payoff is
      coordinates: once a basis is chosen, an abstract vector space behaves exactly like
      <m>\R^n</m>, and an abstract linear transformation becomes a matrix.
    </p>
    <p>
      The chapter closes with two applications in which the vectors are photographs. Two
      pictures of the same cat, alive in one and dead in the other, are combined into the
      superpositions that quantum mechanics describes, using nothing beyond the span,
      basis, and coordinates developed here. Then the question is turned around: instead
      of making new pictures out of old ones, a machine is built that looks at a picture
      and decides what it shows<mdash />a first step into image recognition, powered by
      the same coordinates together with the dot product of the previous chapter.
    </p>
  </introduction>
